speechtext:
With respect to the
earlier portion of my hon. friend's remarks I can only repeat what I have previously said: It never was my intention, and I am assured it never was the intention of the railway, that grade separations in the Montreal scheme should be dealt with any way different front that in which grade separations elsewhere are-dealt with. I have already said I will be glad to allow section 7 or any other relevant section to stand in order to again take up that matter with the legal officers. Really the dispute which has arisen is not a dispute between my hon. friends opposite and myself;
I agree entirely that the intention which has been expressed by hon. gentlemen opposite should be embodied in the law. The quarrel is rather between lawyers, as to whether or not the bill, considered in conjunction with the railway act, does now create that condition. I can just give my hon. friends the assurance that the relevant sections will be held up until I have a further opportunity to take legal advice on the matter. I am quite clear as to the intention both of myself and of the officers of the company, but will consider the matter further. Now, with regard to the other question regarding the bonds issued with respect to the Canadian Northern Railway Company, I find that a statute was passed on June 12, 1914, but that only a small amount of the bonds in question ever were issued. The principal outstanding at present is shown at page 29 of this year's annual report of the Canadian National Railway system, and amounts to only $1,501,000. Apparently it proved difficult or impossible for the old Canadian Northern to finance in the manner contemplated by this bill, and the necessary money apparently was secured from other sources. I am now having inquiries made as to where the money did come from. My own impression is that the money used in the Canadian Northern tunnel terminal construction at Montreal came primarily from government advances subsequently made to the old Canadian Northern Railway Company.
